 <td>0.52</td>\n    <td>0.56</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Nearctic</td>\n    <td>0.39</td>\n    <td>0.72</td>\n    <td> </td>\n    <td>0.54</td>\n    <td>0.45</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Nearctic</td>\n    <td>0.39</td>\n    <td>0.72</td>\n    <td>0.13</td>\n    <td>0.54</td>\n    <td>0.45</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n\n\noccurs in more than one zoological region, we count it as present in each region in which it lives.\n\n# Statistical methods\n\nWe transformed the variables S and AE to common logarithms. Then we performed univariate and bivariate linear regressions and calculated the usual statistics: intercepts, coefficients, probabilities, and R 2 -values. We excluded Hawaii from the regression analyses for Amphibia and Reptilia because these classes are not found in that zoological region.\n\n",
